The chances of a Healer losing their starting pet to a Minetown guard
should be extremely low.  Healers start with a stethoscope, which can
be used once per turn without taking any time, and can be used to
monitor pet HP.  And if the pet HP gets too low, then you can zap it
with a healing spell, throw a healing potion at it, or bash it over
the head with a healing potion.  Remember that if your pet is hit with
a healing potion, that it is automatically restored to full HP.  Also
remember that you can separate a pet from a guard, so you can just do
that if you don't feel comfortable with the pet fighting the guards at
the time.

And because of the stethoscope, a pet dying because of a trap should
also be a rare event.

Knights and Tourists are poor choices for the protection racket:
1)  They don't have infravision, which means that if there is a dark
level above Minetown, then it is hard to keep track of your pet so
that it can protect you.  And a pet you are riding doesn't protect you
very much, so an XL1 knight riding his horse through a dark level
isn't an ideal solution.

2)  Gnomes are hostile towards knights and tourists.  The probability
that a Gnome will have an offensive wand is much higher than the
probability that a healer will lose his pet to a trap.  And a Gnome
with an offensive wand is a big threat to an XL1 character.

3)  They can't monitor their pet's HP very well.  Protection racket
means the pet needs to kill a lot of stuff, and knights and tourists
just aren't that good at letting their pet do all the work.

And by the way, the offensive wand problem is also the reason why
infravision is so important.  For example, if a dwarf on a dark level
is zapping an offensive wand at a healer from far away, then the
healer can easily figure out where the wand is being zapped from, and
thus has a high chance of being able to avoiding the wand.  On the
other hand, if a knight or tourist is being zapped on a dark level
from far away, then it can be really hard for them to continously stay
out of the way of the wand zapping, since they won't be able to figure
out where exactly the wand zapper is on every turn.

